# Building and deploying {{ __node_setup_name }}

This directory was written by `ansible-playbook acme.infra.node_setup`. Running that
again replaces this guide with the one of the installed acme.infra and leaves the
other files alone: they are yours.

| File | What it is | In git |
|---|---|---|
| `{{ __node_setup_service }}.container` | How the service runs on a server (a [Quadlet](https://docs.podman.io/en/latest/markdown/podman-systemd.unit.5.html) file) | Yes |
| `inventory.yml` | The servers to deploy to | Yes |
| `container.env` | The container's settings and secrets | **No** |
| `README.md` | This guide | Yes |

Every command below runs from the project root, on a machine with ansible-core
and the acme.infra collection installed (see acme.infra's README).

## 1. Build the image

```sh
ansible-playbook acme.infra.node_image
```

This needs Podman 3.4 or newer (RHEL 9 or Ubuntu 22.04) and network access to
the container and npm registries. The image lands in `.image/` as
`{{ __node_setup_service }}-<version>.tar`, with the version from package.json. What the project
needs (the `build` and `start:prod` scripts, an optional `.nvmrc`) and the
build options: `ansible-doc -t role acme.infra.node_image`.

## 2. Configure

- `container.env`: the environment variables the app gets, one `KEY=value` per line.
- `{{ __node_setup_service }}.container`: the port on the server (`PublishPort=`, 3000 by
  default), the data directory, restart behaviour. Leave `Image=` as it is: the
  deploy sets it.
- `inventory.yml`: the servers. It lists this machine by default.

## 3. Deploy

```sh
ansible-playbook acme.infra.node_deploy -i deploy/inventory.yml
```

For every server it copies the image of the version in package.json and loads
it into Podman, installs `{{ __node_setup_service }}.container` and `container.env`, and starts
the service, or restarts it when something changed. The service runs rootless,
as the account Ansible logs in with, and starts again after a reboot.

What each server needs, set up once by an administrator:

- RHEL 9.2 or newer with Podman: `sudo dnf install podman`.
- The account the service runs as: an ordinary user, no sudo needed. The deploy
  lets it run services while nobody is logged in (`loginctl enable-linger`).
  Where polkit is not installed, an administrator runs that once instead:
  `sudo loginctl enable-linger <account>`.
- SSH access for that account. Not needed for `localhost`.
- For other machines to reach the service, its port open in the firewall:
  `sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=3000/tcp && sudo firewall-cmd --reload`.

Where things are on the server, in the account's home directory:

| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `~/.config/containers/systemd/{{ __node_setup_service }}.container` | The Quadlet file |
| `~/{{ __node_setup_service }}/container.env` | Settings and secrets |
| `~/{{ __node_setup_service }}/data/` | The app's `/data`, kept across deploys |
| `~/{{ __node_setup_service }}/image.tar` | The image last deployed |

## 4. Check it

On the server, as the account the service runs as:

```sh
systemctl --user status {{ __node_setup_service }}
journalctl --user --unit {{ __node_setup_service }}
curl http://localhost:3000/
```

## In a pipeline

A pipeline runs the same commands. The runner needs Podman for the build and
SSH access to the servers for the deploy.

```sh
ansible-galaxy collection install --force <the acme.infra tarball this project uses>
npm ci && npm run test
ansible-playbook acme.infra.node_image
printf '%s\n' "$CONTAINER_ENV" > deploy/container.env   # from a secret variable
ansible-playbook acme.infra.node_deploy -i deploy/inventory.yml
```

## Updating acme.infra

Install the newer release, then run `ansible-playbook acme.infra.node_setup`
again to get its guide. To compare your files with the newest defaults, move
one away and run the setup: it writes a fresh copy.
